  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381b0d",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "Immature Platelet Fraction (IPF)",
  "code": "IPF",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><strong>Clinical Background&nbsp;</strong></p><p>The IPF test is a measurement of the percentage of immature platelets in the total platelet count. Immature platelets are newly formed platelets that are larger and more reactive than mature platelets. The IPF test is used to evaluate the bone marrow's ability to produce and release platelets.</p><p><strong>Interpretation</strong></p><ul><li>If the IPF value is within the reference range, it indicates that the bone marrow is producing platelets normally, and the patient's platelet count is likely to be stable.</li><li>If the IPF value is higher than the reference range, it suggests that the bone marrow is producing more immature platelets in response to the increased demand for platelets. This can occur in conditions such as thrombocytopenia, bone marrow disorders, or after chemotherapy.</li><li>If the IPF value is lower than the reference range, it suggests that the bone marrow is not producing enough immature platelets, which can be seen in conditions such as aplastic anemia or bone marrow failure.</li></ul><p><strong>​<br></strong></p>",
